<BaseLayout title="01. The Temporal Gradient" description="The fundamental driver of the Temporal Decoupling framework.">
  <article class="container" style="padding-top: 4rem; padding-bottom: 4rem; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1>01. The Temporal Gradient</h1>
    <p class="lead">The Temporal Gradient is the difference in "chronometric density" between the static Timeless Sea (TSC) and the sweeping Timeline hypersurface (Σ). This gradient is the engine that creates the flow of time and the resistance we perceive as mass.</p>

    <h2>The Engine of Reality</h2>
    <p>Imagine the Timeless Sea as a vast, static ocean. Our Timeline is a sheet of glass sweeping through it. The <strong>Gradient</strong> is the friction between the two. Where the glass moves fast, the friction is low (sparse space). Where the glass slows down or bunches up, the friction is high (dense matter).</p>
    
    <ul style="line-height: 1.8; color: #334155;">
      <li><strong>Flow Lines:</strong> These are not paths of motion, but the "motion blur" of static TSC entities being smeared across our moving timeline by the gradient.</li>
      <li><strong>Mass:</strong> Hydrodynamic resistance caused by the depth of intersection with the gradient.</li>
      <li><strong>Charge:</strong> Asymmetric fluid vortices created by the tilt of intersection.</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin-top: 3rem; padding: 1.5rem; background: #f8fafc; border-left: 4px solid #2563eb; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #0f172a;">Next Step</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 1rem; color: #475569;">Now that we understand the gradient, we can look at how it structures time into the A-B-C-D architecture.</p>
      <a href="/framework/v7-overview/" style="display: inline-block; padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: 500;">View V7 Architecture →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>